1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,020
Speaker 1: Hey everybody, Peter Zion here coming to you from Colorado. It is the 17th of July and it is the last day of the Republican National Convention, which I've been watching pretty closely because the Republican Party is in flux.

2
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:21,480
Speaker 1: For those of you who've been watching me for a while, you know that I've been saying for a couple years, several years now, that we're in the late party structure for what is called the sixth party structure.

3
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:29,800
Speaker 1: Basically, every generation or two, American politics goes into a period of flux where the factions that make up our parties move around.

4
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:38,980
Speaker 1: And for the last few years, we've been in that process. It's happening for both of the parties, but the Republicans are further along, so it's been the one I've been following most closely.

5
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:42,620
Speaker 1: Donald Trump is obviously part of this process and has been speeding it along.

6
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:49,220
Speaker 1: And now with the convention, we're starting to get a pretty good idea of what the next possible iteration of the Republicans are.

7
00:00:49,220 --> 00:01:07,380
Speaker 1: Quick review. So most people associate the Republicans with the degree of social conservatism, international involvement, relatively hawkish positions on foreign affairs, especially military affairs, and a fairly pro-business outlook for businesses of all size.

8
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:20,260
Speaker 1: Every plank of that is now being challenged and rewritten by Team Trump as American society, demographics, trade, technology, and everything else is evolving.

9
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:25,520
Speaker 1: It makes sense. Whether or not this new structure that's evolving is the one that is going to stick remains to be seen.

10
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:29,720
Speaker 1: But we now have a pretty good idea of what the Trump Republican Party would be.

11
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:37,620
Speaker 1: Three big changes. The first one is a little bit of a surprise, a softer position on some social conservative issues like gays and abortion.

12
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:44,440
Speaker 1: Basically, the Trump Party is now saying that these are issues that should be decided by the states rather than the national level.

13
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:49,020
Speaker 1: And there's a lot of hardcore conservatives, social conservatives, who are really pretty unhappy with that.

14
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:57,000
Speaker 1: But Donald Trump is betting that his cult of personality-like hold over the Republican Party is going to be sufficient, that no one can outflank him.

15
00:01:57,460 --> 00:02:05,740
Speaker 1: And considering that he sailed to the nomination without even showing up at any of the primaries, that's probably a safe bet, at least for this election cycle.

16
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:12,400
Speaker 1: The second one, of course, is something that's near and dear to my heart, and it's a shift from international involvement to isolationism.

17
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:15,460
Speaker 1: The idea that the problems over there are problems over there.

18
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:18,520
Speaker 1: We should just stick over here, but still arm ourselves to the teeth.

19
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:24,080
Speaker 1: We saw a lot of this in Trump's first term, where he might talk tough on countries with Russia and China.

20
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:29,240
Speaker 1: But really, on every major issue, whether it's trade or security, he basically just let them do whatever they want.

21
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:35,660
Speaker 1: If it couldn't be negotiated and stuck to in a single afternoon of talks, he really wasn't interested.

22
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:44,340
Speaker 1: We saw that, of course, with Ukraine, and we've also seen it with trade policy with China, where we got this phase one trade deal, but then Trump couldn't bother to enforce it.

23
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:47,520
Speaker 1: So the Chinese basically just walked all over the United States as regards trade.

24
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:52,040
Speaker 1: Things like that are definitely being codified into the Republican Party's new platform.

25
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:57,080
Speaker 1: And then the third one has to do with business versus labor.

26
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:06,240
Speaker 1: For the past several decades, really going back to the 1930s, the Republican Party has always sided with the business community over labor.

27
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:12,500
Speaker 1: But one of the highlight speakers of the convention at this time was none other than the representative of the Teamsters Union,

28
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:17,860
Speaker 1: which is probably the most militant and throwback of America's unions.

29
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:22,180
Speaker 1: And he talked about the Chamber of Commerce basically being a welfare club for businesses.

30
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:28,800
Speaker 1: And this is something that was on the stage of the Republican National Convention.

31
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,860
Speaker 1: I mean, for a while there, I was wondering if I was watching the right convention.

32
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:38,760
Speaker 1: These are issues that are usually trumpeted by not just the Democrats, but the really leftist group of Democrats, like, say, the Squad.

33
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:43,200
Speaker 1: And now it's becoming core of the Republican Party platform.

34
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:49,780
Speaker 1: In essence, we are entering a golden age for organized labor in the United States.

35
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:53,080
Speaker 1: The United States is in the process of doubling the size of its industrial plant.

36
00:03:53,220 --> 00:03:54,980
Speaker 1: Most of those jobs are blue collar.

37
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:59,640
Speaker 1: And so they're very amenable to be organized into labor unions.

38
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:01,880
Speaker 1: The Teamsters, of course, are all for that.

39
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:12,000
Speaker 1: And what the Trump Republican Party seems to be doing is basically ditching the entire business community and going whole hog towards organized labor.

40
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:21,800
Speaker 1: And among the new things that are in the Republican Party's platform are basically starting to challenge things like right-to-work laws in places like the American South and Texas

41
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:27,760
Speaker 1: that prevent or at least dissuade unions from forming in the first place.

42
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:31,720
Speaker 1: It's now the official Republican Party platform that that's a bad idea.

43
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:34,680
Speaker 1: Now, will this new trifecta stick?

44
00:04:34,860 --> 00:04:36,360
Speaker 1: It's way too soon to tell.

45
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,940
Speaker 1: And the old Reaganite and Lankonian Republicans are not dead yet.

46
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:43,820
Speaker 1: There were some hilarious moments on stage from my point of view.

47
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:49,100
Speaker 1: Ron DeSantis, who ran for president, of course, governor of Florida, he was just angry.

48
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:50,880
Speaker 1: And it was pretty obvious.

49
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:55,140
Speaker 1: But I would say that the highlight here for awkwardness goes to Nikki Haley,

50
00:04:55,140 --> 00:04:59,240
Speaker 1: who came in second in the primaries against Donald Trump, still lost, of course.

51
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:01,500
Speaker 1: She came on stage to endorse Trump.

52
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:07,120
Speaker 1: And not only was she booed when she showed up, but her endorsement speech was really awkward.

53
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:16,380
Speaker 1: It was kind of like a woman being asked to toast her ex-husband who had an affair with the secretary and was now marrying her.

54
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,240
Speaker 1: It was that level of awkwardness.

55
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:27,440
Speaker 1: Anyway, Nikki Haley has already joined a think tank to reimagine what the next version of conservatism looks like in the United States

56
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:30,260
Speaker 1: now that the old Republican Party is truly and well dead.

57
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:34,560
Speaker 1: And Mike Pence has formed a think tank to do the same thing.

58
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:40,680
Speaker 1: Now, I'm not suggesting that one of these three visions, Pence or Haley or Trump, is the one that's ultimately going to stick.

59
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:48,200
Speaker 1: But I'm just highlighting that right now for this election cycle, we have a very different Republican platform and a very different Republican Party.

60
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:50,040
Speaker 1: Everything is still in flux.

61
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:51,560
Speaker 1: This is not the final form.

62
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:54,140
Speaker 1: We won't get that probably until the next presidential cycle.

63
00:05:54,140 --> 00:06:01,080
Speaker 1: And there's a lot of folks who have irons in the fire to try to figure out what that is going to be.
